---
// firma2-measure-rollup-preview — the lower of the two preview panels on a
// performance measure's setup page. It answers one question, and only that one:
// what has this measure actually accumulated across every project reporting it?
//
// WHY A PREVIEW PANEL AND NOT A REPORT. Setup is where an author decides whether
// a measure is worth defining the way they are defining it, and that decision is
// unmakeable in the abstract. "Acres treated with prescribed fire" reads fine as
// a definition and turns out to be one project, 538 of 1,300 acres, four years
// in. Seeing the portfolio it already rolls up is what turns an abstract
// vocabulary decision into a concrete one — so the panel sits under the fields
// that produce it, on the same screen, collapsible because it is context rather
// than a control.
//
// IT IS LIVE, AND THAT IS THE POINT OF THE SCREEN. The layout's whole claim is
// that the previews answer as you define the measure. A panel rendered once from
// the seed record and never updated makes that claim false — the rail edits, the
// preview sits there showing yesterday's answer, and a reviewer learns to
// distrust both panels. So this one listens on the frozen MEASURE_CHANGE_EVENT
// contract in src/lib/measure-draft.ts and recomputes.
//
// IT RECOMPUTES EVERYTHING, DELIBERATELY. getMeasureRollup() is a pure function
// of the record; asking "did this edit touch a field the rollup reads?" is a
// second, hand-maintained copy of that dependency list, and the first time
// someone adds a field to the rollup and forgets to add it here, the panel
// starts lying again in a way nothing can see. One call, every change.
//
// RENAMING GENUINELY CHANGES THE ANSWER, AND THE PANEL MUST SAY SO. The rollup
// JOINS ON MEASURE NAME, exactly (see the module header). So editing the name
// detaches the measure from every project reporting it, usually to nothing, and
// this panel drops to its empty state mid-rename. That is not a rendering bug to
// paper over with the last good numbers — the last good numbers would be the
// only actual lie available here. It is the join being honest, and showing it is
// arguably the most useful thing this panel does: renaming a measure in a live
// catalog has a cost, and this is where an admin can see it before saving.
//
// IT COMPUTES NOTHING ITSELF. getMeasureRollup() owns the join between the
// measure catalog and the per-project reported values, including the rule that
// the match is by EXACT name and that a near-miss returns an empty rollup rather
// than a fuzzy total. That rule is documented once, in the module's header, next
// to the two datasets it reconciles. This file deliberately does not import
// firma2-project-detail: a second path to the same numbers is a second answer
// that can disagree with the first.
//
// THREE STATES, ALL REAL, ALL DELIBERATE — and the middle one is the reason the
// empty state is conditioned on `series.length` rather than on `reportedTotal`:
//
//   1. Reporting, with values.  acres-treated-prescribed-fire: 1 project,
//      2023–2026, 538 of 1,300 acres.
//   2. Reporting, nothing reported yet.  fish-passage-barriers-removed: 2
//      projects, 0 of 7 barriers, because both are still in planning. The chart
//      draws two zero-height bars against a real axis, which is the honest
//      picture — projects ARE attached and the answer so far is nothing. An
//      empty state here would say "no projects report this", which is false.
//   3. Nothing reports it.  plant-survival-rate, sediment-load-reduced,
//      cost-per-acre-treated, and every unnamed draft. `series` is empty, and
//      esa-empty-state is the lego for exactly that.
//
// The `series.length` test is now made in TWO places — the frontmatter below for
// the static first paint, and paint() in the script for every answer after it.
// Both read the same field off the same function's return, so they cannot
// disagree about which state is which; and the script paints once on load, so
// the client's answer is the only one on screen after hydration either way.
//
// BOTH STATE-1/2 AND STATE-3 MARKUP IS ALWAYS RENDERED, one of them `hidden`.
// A transition between them is then a `hidden` flip, not a subtree build: the
// stats, the note and the data table survive as the same nodes with new text,
// the esa-collapsible frame is never touched, and there is no markup this file
// can only produce from JavaScript. The one exception is the chart — see the
// script.
//
// ALL THE BARS ARE THE SAME HEIGHT, AND THAT IS THE DATA TELLING THE TRUTH.
// Every measure in this catalog is reported by a single project, and a project
// stores one cumulative figure plus its implementation window — so the module
// spreads that total evenly and each year gets an identical share. The chart is
// therefore a picture of WHEN a measure has been accruing, not of how the pace
// changed. That claim is not left to be inferred from flat bars: it is stated in
// one line under the chart and wired to the chart as its description. See NOTE.
import EsaCollapsible from '@esa/ecology/esa-collapsible.astro';
import EsaStat from '@esa/ecology/esa-stat.astro';
import EsaEmptyState from '@esa/ecology/esa-empty-state.astro';
import { getMeasureRollup } from '../../data/firma2-measure-rollup';
import type { PerformanceMeasureDefinition } from '../../data/firma2-performance-measures';

interface Props {
  /**
   * The measure this panel rolls up — the SEED record. Only its name, unit and
   * precision are read. After load the panel follows the working record off
   * MEASURE_CHANGE_EVENT instead; this prop is the answer before the first
   * event, and the answer for a reader with no JavaScript.
   */
  measure: PerformanceMeasureDefinition;
}

const { measure } = Astro.props;
const rollup = getMeasureRollup(measure);
const hasSeries = rollup.series.length > 0;

// A LOCAL Intl.NumberFormat, and the one case src/lib/format.ts does not cover.
// The shared formatters are shared because a page showing "$1,240,000" in one
// section and "$1.24M" in another is how that file's header opens. This one
// cannot be shared: its precision comes off the RECORD — decimalPlaces is 0 for
// barriers, 1 for acres of riparian habitat, 2 for stream miles — so there is no
// single instance to hoist. Pinning both bounds to the same value is what stops
// a derived 0.666… from rendering more decimals than the measure would ever
// accept from a reporter. It is rebuilt per paint in the script for the same
// reason: decimalPlaces is one of the fields being edited.
const number = new Intl.NumberFormat('en-US', {
  minimumFractionDigits: measure.decimalPlaces,
  maximumFractionDigits: measure.decimalPlaces,
});

// The unit travels with the figure rather than being hoisted into a label,
// because it is not constant across measures ("acres", "barriers", "plants",
// "tons per year") and is EMPTY for Percent and Currency measures, which carry
// their own mark. Concatenating on a blank string degrades to a bare number
// instead of a stray space — no branch needed at the call sites below.
const unitSuffix = rollup.unit ? ` ${rollup.unit}` : '';
// Sentence case, because it is used as an axis title and a column header, both
// of which start a phrase. "tons per year" -> "Tons per year".
const unitTitle = rollup.unit ? rollup.unit.charAt(0).toUpperCase() + rollup.unit.slice(1) : '';

// The chart's accessible name, and it names what the chart SHOWS. esa-chart
// warns at runtime when `label` is absent because AG Charts then generates
// "chart, 1 series" — a name that describes the shape of the object and nothing
// about the subject. The measure's own name is what a reader arrived with, so it
// has to track the name the reader is currently editing, not the one this page
// was built from. The script re-applies it on every paint.
const chartLabel = `${measure.name} reported by year`;

// Scoped to the measure so two panels on one screen never collide on the IDREF
// esa-chart resolves for its description. Slugs are already URL-safe.
const noteId = `firma2-rollup-note-${measure.slug}`;

// THE SEED RECORD, SERIALIZED — not the seed ROLLUP.
//
// The old version of this file shipped the computed rows in a data- attribute
// and the script only had to hand them to the chart. That cannot survive going
// live: the rows are a function of a record the user is still editing, and the
// set of records they can produce is every string they can type.
//
// Serializing the RECORD instead, and calling the same getMeasureRollup() in the
// browser, keeps the one-function rule the header opens with. The alternative —
// precomputing a name→rollup table at build time, since the join key is a finite
// set of names in static data — was measured and rejected on two counts. It is
// not actually finite: `decimalPlaces` rounds the series, so the table is names ×
// precisions, and it would have to be rebuilt from a re-derivation rather than
// from getMeasureRollup itself, which is exactly the second disagreeing answer
// the header bans. And the saving is nothing: bundling getMeasureRollup with its
// two data modules costs 31.5 KB minified / 9.3 KB gzipped (esbuild, --bundle
// --minify), against the 353 KB gzipped of ag-charts-community that any page
// showing a chart already pays. It is 2.6% of the chart library, on a page that
// only exists to render the chart. Serialized on the element rather than passed
// through `define:vars`, which forces `is:inline` and would leave the bare
// specifiers in the script below unresolvable by the browser.
const measurePayload = JSON.stringify(measure);

// PLURALIZATION, spelled out rather than bolted on with "(s)". One project
// reporting is the common case in this catalog, not an edge case, and
// firma2-measure-readiness makes the same call for its outstanding-field count.
const projectsLabel = `${rollup.projectCount === 1 ? 'Project' : 'Projects'} reporting`;
---

<!-- "Reported across the portfolio" — front-loaded on the word that separates
     this panel from the one above it, which previews how the measure will look
     to a reporter. "Portfolio" is this spoke's own word for the whole project
     set (it is what firma2-project-grid is called in prose), so it names the
     scope with a term already on the reader's screen elsewhere.

     THE COLLAPSIBLE IS NEVER RE-RENDERED. It is a native <details>, so its
     open/closed state is DOM state on this element — and every update below
     happens strictly inside it, on nodes that already exist. Nothing this
     component does can collapse the panel, reset it, or move the scroll
     position, because nothing replaces the element that holds those.

     No leading icon. esa-collapsible takes one and the house sanctions icons on
     section headers — but the only glyph in esa-icon's registry that is close is
     `trending-up`, and nothing here trends: the bars are flat by construction.
     A glyph that asserts a shape the data does not have is worse than no glyph. -->
<EsaCollapsible title="Reported across the portfolio" open>
  <!-- The panel root the script binds to. `data-firma2-rollup-slug` is the
       address: MEASURE_CHANGE_EVENT is dispatched on `document` and carries ONE
       record, so a screen stacking two of these panels would otherwise repaint
       both from whichever measure was edited. The slug is not an editable field,
       so it is a stable key for the life of the page. -->
  <div
    class="firma2-measure-rollup"
    data-firma2-rollup-panel
    data-firma2-rollup-slug={measure.slug}
    data-firma2-rollup-measure={measurePayload}
  >
    <!-- States 1 and 2. `stack` rather than nothing, because inserting this
         wrapper takes the children out of esa-collapsible's body flex and its
         1rem gap with them; `.stack`'s default --gap is --spacing-400, the same
         token the collapsible body uses, so the rhythm is unchanged rather than
         re-guessed. -->
    <div class="firma2-measure-rollup__view stack" data-firma2-rollup-view="data" hidden={!hasSeries}>
      <!-- The totals come FIRST, above the chart. The question the panel exists
           to answer is "how much, out of how much, from how many projects" —
           that is a number, and the dataviz skill is explicit that a headline
           figure belongs in a stat tile rather than being read off a bar. The
           chart is the follow-up question (when did it accrue), so it sits
           second. esa-stat is the lego for a value/label pair; it renders its
           own display-sm value and label-md label, so there is nothing to style.

           `cluster`, not a bespoke flex row: two stats that wrap to a second
           line at narrow widths is exactly what the primitive does.

           The wrapper div around each esa-stat is an ADDRESS, not a layout. The
           script has to retarget the text inside these on every paint, and
           `querySelectorAll('.esa-stat')[0]` would silently retarget itself the
           day a third stat is added in front. See the legoGap note in the
           script: esa-stat is an .astro lego with no slots, so its rendered
           light-DOM BEM classes are the only handle a runtime update has. -->
      <div class="cluster" data-gap="xl">
        <!-- Value carries the unit; `sub` carries the denominator. Splitting
             expected into a third tile would give equal visual weight to a
             figure that only means anything AGAINST the first one — "538 acres"
             under "of 1,300 expected" is the comparison in one read.

             `sub` is always passed, never conditionally: esa-stat renders the
             sub node only when the prop is truthy, and a node that is absent at
             build time is a node the script cannot fill later. Every measure
             with a series has an expected total, so there is no case where this
             is empty anyway. -->
        <div data-firma2-rollup-stat="total">
          <EsaStat
            value={`${number.format(rollup.reportedTotal)}${unitSuffix}`}
            label="Reported to date"
            sub={`of ${number.format(rollup.expectedTotal)} expected`}
          />
        </div>
        <div data-firma2-rollup-stat="projects">
          <EsaStat value={rollup.projectCount} label={projectsLabel} />
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- COLUMNS, not a line, and the form was chosen from the dataviz skill's
           job table rather than from habit. The reader's job here is comparing
           the magnitude of discrete annual buckets, which is the bar row. A line
           would be worse than merely unfashionable: it draws a continuous path
           between year points and so asserts values BETWEEN them, and there are
           none — a project reports a cumulative figure, and the module buckets
           it by whole years. Bars leave the gaps as gaps.

           ONE SERIES, so no legend (esa-chart only enables one past a single
           series) and no direct labels: "a number on every data point" is in the
           skill's anti-pattern list, and with 4–6 identical bars it would be six
           copies of one number. The y-axis ticks carry the values on screen and
           the data table below carries them exactly.

           COLOUR is the categorical family's slot 1 — esa-chart's default scale,
           resolved from --color-background-dataviz-categorical-1 in JS because a
           canvas cannot read a custom property. Slot 1 is the only correct
           answer for a single series: the sequential family encodes magnitude in
           lightness, and magnitude here is already carried by bar length, so a
           ramp would double-encode it and burn the one free channel (the skill
           calls that out by name as "a value-ramp on nominal categories").

           THE <esa-chart> ELEMENT IS NOT HERE AT BUILD TIME. It is minted into
           this slot the first time the panel has a series, and then kept
           forever. The reason is a 353 KB gzipped one: esa-chart dynamically
           imports ag-charts-community from firstUpdated(), so an element that
           exists downloads the library and an element that does not, does not.
           Rendering it unconditionally would make every measure with no rollup —
           which is most of the catalog, and every new draft — pay for a chart it
           is not showing. The chart is 100% JavaScript either way, so nothing is
           lost for a reader without it; the data table below is the state that
           survives with scripting off, and it is real markup. -->
      <div class="firma2-measure-rollup__chart" data-firma2-rollup-chart></div>

      <!-- NOTE — the provenance of the per-year split, stated once, plainly, and
           immediately under the marks it qualifies.

           This is NOT narration about the page, which design-principles bans: it
           does not tell the reader what the data shows or how to feel about it.
           It is a statement about how these particular numbers came to exist,
           and without it the chart makes a claim the data cannot support — a bar
           per year reads as a year's reported figure, and it is not one. A
           reader who takes four flat bars as four annual reports has been misled
           by the rendering, not by the source, so the correction belongs at the
           rendering.

           It is wired to the chart with `described-by`: esa-chart resolves the
           IDREF in the light DOM and applies it by element reflection, which is
           the one association that crosses a shadow boundary. So the caveat is
           announced with the figure rather than only sitting near it. The
           sentence is invariant across measures — the split rule does not depend
           on the record — so the script never rewrites it, and the id it is
           wired by never changes. The association therefore cannot be broken by
           an update, which is the failure mode a rebuilt description would have.

           Primary ink, not the secondary grey that `body-sm` usually wears here.
           A qualification a reader must not skip should not be styled as the
           thing on the page most safely skipped. -->
      <p id={noteId} data-firma2-rollup-note class="firma2-measure-rollup__note typography-body-sm">
        Yearly figures are estimated: each project&rsquo;s running total, split evenly
        across its reporting years.
      </p>

      <!-- THE TABLE IS THE CHART'S TEXT ALTERNATIVE, and it is not optional.
           AG Charts ships no table, no figcaption and no offscreen text — the
           hub's own component doc scores that as a straight FAIL and says the
           fix is a composition, not a prop, because a paired disclosure gives a
           sighted keyboard user a real affordance instead of an offscreen table
           only a screen reader can find. There is a second reason in the hub's
           notes: several slots of the categorical palette sit under 3:1 against
           a light surface, and the validator passes them only WITH relief —
           visible labels or a table view. This is that relief.

           SO IT UPDATES WITH THE CHART, IN THE SAME PAINT. A text alternative
           that lags the figure it duplicates is not a weaker alternative, it is
           a false one — and it is false only for the reader who cannot check it
           against the canvas. paint() writes the caption, the unit header and
           every row from the same rollup object that feeds the chart, in one
           synchronous pass, so there is no interval in which the two disagree.

           The stats above do not discharge this. They are portfolio totals; the
           chart plots a per-year series, and no total contains a year's figure.

           `flush` so it reads as a quiet row inside this panel rather than a
           second card boxed inside the first. Its own open/closed state is also
           native <details> state on an element nothing below replaces — a reader
           who opened the table stays looking at the table across every edit.
           "View as data" is the hub's own string for this pairing, kept verbatim
           — an approved pattern is canon, and a reader who has met it on one
           chart should meet it on all. -->
      <EsaCollapsible title="View as data" flush>
        <!-- bcn-lego-checked: no esa- lego renders a table. Walked the catalog at
             node_modules/@esa/ecology/src/components/ — there is no esa-table;
             esa-stat holds one figure, esa-link-column is a list of links, and
             esa-chart is the thing this exists to make readable. AG Grid is this
             spoke's tabular precedent but is scoped to the portfolio table:
             search, filters and CSV export around four rows of derived figures
             is chrome with nothing to operate on. Beacon is not cloned on this
             machine, so tier 2 was skipped. A native <table> with real <th
             scope> IS the semantic primitive here — that association is the
             whole point of rendering it — so the honest answer is markup. It
             stays a one-off rather than a bcn- component for the same reason
             firma2-project-funding's does: a reusable table is a hub concern. -->
        <table class="firma2-measure-rollup__table">
          <!-- Named for the chart it duplicates, so the two are recognisably the
               same data — which means it carries the measure NAME, and so it is
               repainted with everything else. Hidden because printing it under a
               disclosure row that already says "View as data" would be the same
               words twice. -->
          <caption class="visually-hidden" data-firma2-rollup-caption>{chartLabel}</caption>
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th scope="col">Year</th>
              <!-- The unit lives in the header rather than being repeated down
                   the column: 40 rendered units in a four-row table is noise,
                   and the column is homogeneous by construction. -->
              <th scope="col" class="firma2-measure-rollup__num" data-firma2-rollup-unit-header>
                {unitTitle ? `Reported (${rollup.unit})` : 'Reported'}
              </th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody data-firma2-rollup-rows>
            {rollup.series.map((year) => (
              <tr>
                <!-- The year names the row, so it is a row header: it is what a
                     screen reader reads back to place you when you arrow across
                     into the figure. -->
                <th scope="row">{year.year}</th>
                <td class="firma2-measure-rollup__num">{number.format(year.value)}</td>
              </tr>
            ))}
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </EsaCollapsible>
    </div>

    <!-- State 3. The title states what is absent as a fact, and states the
         RIGHT fact: nothing is wrong with the measure, it simply is not attached
         to any project yet — which is where every measure starts, including the
         unnamed draft, and where every measure goes the moment its name stops
         matching. The description is the one recovery action, imperative and
         short, per esa-empty-state's own contract. `sm` matches every other
         empty state in this spoke's card sections — and keeps the height this
         state costs small, which matters now that it is a state the panel enters
         and leaves rather than one it was built in.

         The wrapper exists to carry `hidden`: esa-empty-state is an .astro
         component and does not forward arbitrary attributes to its root. -->
    <div data-firma2-rollup-view="empty" hidden={hasSeries}>
      <EsaEmptyState
        title="No projects report this measure"
        description="Add this measure to a project to start collecting values."
        size="sm"
      />
    </div>
  </div>
</EsaCollapsible>

<script>
  import '@esa/ecology/esa-chart';
  import { MEASURE_CHANGE_EVENT, type MeasureChangeDetail } from '../../lib/measure-draft';
  import { getMeasureRollup } from '../../data/firma2-measure-rollup';
  import type { PerformanceMeasureDefinition } from '../../data/firma2-performance-measures';

  /*
   * WHAT THIS SCRIPT IS FOR: keeping the panel equal to the record the user is
   * editing. It listens; it never dispatches. The config rail owns the record
   * and owns MEASURE_CHANGE_EVENT, and a preview that wrote back to the thing it
   * previews would be a loop with no obvious end.
   *
   * THE ACCESSIBILITY OF THE UPDATE ITSELF, decided rather than defaulted.
   * This panel changes while focus is in a field somewhere else, which is the
   * shape of a status message (SC 4.1.3). The accessibility skill's
   * STATUS-MESSAGE contract is explicit that a live region is the LAST of four
   * options, and it is the wrong one here for a specific reason, not a general
   * distaste:
   *
   *   - The content is not transient. Everything this panel can say stays on the
   *     screen, inside a named disclosure, re-readable at any time — which is
   *     precisely what an announcement is not. Nothing has to be caught in
   *     flight, because nothing goes away.
   *   - Announcing it would be actively worse than silence. The field that moves
   *     this panel most is the NAME field, and the join is exact — so every
   *     keystroke of a rename passes through a non-matching name. A live region
   *     would read "no projects report this measure" into the ear of someone in
   *     the middle of typing a word, over and over. That is the skill's
   *     filtering anti-pattern verbatim: wrapping results in aria-live so every
   *     keystroke reads the list back.
   *   - The transition-into-empty exception does not apply either. The skill
   *     reserves an assertive announcement for a search that empties out,
   *     because that user is typing into nothing and needs to correct. Here
   *     "empty" is not an error and needs no correction — it is the correct
   *     answer for a measure not yet attached to a project, and it is the state
   *     the user is deliberately passing through on the way to a new name.
   *
   * So option 1 in the contract is the fit: an instructional cue, set once, on
   * the control the user is operating. By construction that cue lives on the
   * rail's fields, not in this file — see the note returned to the page
   * controller. What this file owes the same reader instead is that the panel is
   * never STALE when they arrive at it: the chart's accessible name tracks the
   * current measure name, and the data table is repainted in the same pass as
   * the chart, so navigating here at any moment finds the current answer
   * correctly named. A panel that is always right when read is the thing an
   * announcement would only be approximating.
   */

  /** esa-chart's element class is not imported for typing — the module is
   *  imported for its REGISTRATION side effect, which is all a custom element
   *  needs to exist in the document. */
  type ChartElement = HTMLElement & Record<string, unknown>;

  /*
   * DEBOUNCE. Long enough to sit out a fast typist's inter-keystroke interval
   * (~120–200ms), short enough that the panel still reads as a response to the
   * edit rather than a page that reloaded. It is not a performance hedge — one
   * getMeasureRollup() over 24 projects is trivial — it is a STABILITY one: a
   * rename walks through non-matching names, so an undebounced panel would drop
   * to the empty state and climb back out on nearly every keystroke, and the
   * height would move with it. Settling once per pause is the difference between
   * a preview and a strobe.
   */
  const SETTLE_MS = 250;

  const setUp = (panel: HTMLElement): void => {
    const slug = panel.dataset.firma2RollupSlug ?? '';
    const seedJson = panel.dataset.firma2RollupMeasure;
    if (!seedJson) return;

    const dataView = panel.querySelector<HTMLElement>('[data-firma2-rollup-view="data"]');
    const emptyView = panel.querySelector<HTMLElement>('[data-firma2-rollup-view="empty"]');
    const chartSlot = panel.querySelector<HTMLElement>('[data-firma2-rollup-chart]');
    const caption = panel.querySelector<HTMLElement>('[data-firma2-rollup-caption]');
    const unitHeader = panel.querySelector<HTMLElement>('[data-firma2-rollup-unit-header]');
    const rows = panel.querySelector<HTMLElement>('[data-firma2-rollup-rows]');
    const noteId = panel.querySelector<HTMLElement>('[data-firma2-rollup-note]')?.id ?? '';

    /*
     * legoGap — esa-stat is a presentational .astro lego with no slots and no
     * Lit counterpart, so there is no supported way to change a stat's value
     * after render. Reaching for its light-DOM BEM classes is the least-bad
     * handle available: they are stable, they are what the lego's own stylesheet
     * targets, and the alternative is hand-rolling a value/label pair, which is
     * the exact reinvention esa-stat exists to prevent. Worth a /request-lego
     * for a Lit `esa-stat` (or slot support on the .astro one) so a live figure
     * has a real contract. Queried through the address wrapper rather than by
     * position so adding a stat cannot silently repoint these.
     */
    const statPart = (stat: string, part: string): HTMLElement | null =>
      panel.querySelector<HTMLElement>(`[data-firma2-rollup-stat="${stat}"] .esa-stat__${part}`);

    const totalValue = statPart('total', 'value');
    const totalSub = statPart('total', 'sub');
    const countValue = statPart('projects', 'value');
    const countLabel = statPart('projects', 'label');

    if (!dataView || !emptyView || !chartSlot || !rows) return;

    /*
     * ONE CHART INSTANCE, EVER. Minted on first need (see the markup for why it
     * is not server-rendered), then retained for the life of the page — hidden
     * with its subtree when the panel falls back to the empty state, never
     * removed. Removal is not a neutral act here: esa-chart destroys its AG
     * Charts instance in disconnectedCallback, so a rename that bounced through
     * the empty state would tear down and rebuild the whole chart, and a
     * re-entrant rebuild is the thrash this refactor exists to avoid. Hidden and
     * reused, it stays connected, keeps its instance, and comes back on a
     * property assignment.
     */
    let chart: ChartElement | null = null;
    const ensureChart = (): ChartElement => {
      if (chart) return chart;
      const el = document.createElement('esa-chart') as ChartElement;
      // Attributes, not properties, for the two ARIA inputs — mirroring what the
      // markup used to declare, and observed by esa-chart's `static properties`.
      // `described-by` is set once because the note's id never changes.
      if (noteId) el.setAttribute('described-by', noteId);
      chartSlot.appendChild(el);
      chart = el;
      return el;
    };

    const paint = (measure: PerformanceMeasureDefinition): void => {
      const rollup = getMeasureRollup(measure);
      // STATE 2 IS NOT STATE 3. Conditioned on the series, not on the total —
      // two projects reporting zero barriers so far is a real chart of zeroes,
      // and calling it "no projects report this measure" would be false. Same
      // test as the frontmatter, off the same field.
      const hasSeries = rollup.series.length > 0;

      /*
       * VISIBILITY FIRST, then content. The chart is created and measured inside
       * dataView; a `display: none` ancestor makes its container 0×0, and AG
       * Charts would lay out against that. Flipping `hidden` before any chart
       * work means the box has its real width by the time Lit's update runs.
       */
      dataView.hidden = !hasSeries;
      emptyView.hidden = hasSeries;
      if (!hasSeries) return;

      // Rebuilt per paint: precision is one of the fields being edited, so the
      // formatter is part of the answer rather than a constant. See frontmatter.
      const number = new Intl.NumberFormat('en-US', {
        minimumFractionDigits: measure.decimalPlaces,
        maximumFractionDigits: measure.decimalPlaces,
      });
      const unitSuffix = rollup.unit ? ` ${rollup.unit}` : '';
      const unitTitle = rollup.unit
        ? rollup.unit.charAt(0).toUpperCase() + rollup.unit.slice(1)
        : '';
      const chartLabel = `${measure.name} reported by year`;

      if (totalValue) {
        totalValue.textContent = `${number.format(rollup.reportedTotal)}${unitSuffix}`;
      }
      if (totalSub) totalSub.textContent = `of ${number.format(rollup.expectedTotal)} expected`;
      if (countValue) countValue.textContent = String(rollup.projectCount);
      // The singular/plural branch, kept identical to the frontmatter's. One
      // project reporting is the common case in this catalog, not an edge case.
      if (countLabel) {
        countLabel.textContent = `${rollup.projectCount === 1 ? 'Project' : 'Projects'} reporting`;
      }

      // THE TEXT ALTERNATIVE, in the same pass. Caption carries the measure name
      // so the table and the chart are recognisably the same data; the header
      // carries the unit so the column does not repeat it forty times.
      if (caption) caption.textContent = chartLabel;
      if (unitHeader) {
        unitHeader.textContent = unitTitle ? `Reported (${rollup.unit})` : 'Reported';
      }

      /*
       * Rows are built with createElement rather than an HTML string — no
       * injection surface for a name the user typed, and `textContent` is the
       * only writer. replaceChildren() swaps the whole body in one mutation, so
       * a screen reader walking the table can never land between an old row and
       * a new one.
       *
       * The class here is deliberately NOT scoped by Astro: nodes minted at
       * runtime never receive the compile-time `data-astro-cid-*` attribute, so
       * the table's rules are declared :global() below. See the style block.
       */
      const next = document.createDocumentFragment();
      for (const year of rollup.series) {
        const tr = document.createElement('tr');
        const th = document.createElement('th');
        th.scope = 'row';
        th.textContent = String(year.year);
        const td = document.createElement('td');
        td.className = 'firma2-measure-rollup__num';
        td.textContent = number.format(year.value);
        tr.append(th, td);
        next.append(tr);
      }
      rows.replaceChildren(next);

      /*
       * THE CHART IS UPDATED, NOT REBUILT. `data`, `series` and `options` are
       * PROPERTIES on esa-chart — assigning them marks the element dirty, and
       * esa-chart's updated() routes a change in any of those to
       * chart.update(), which is AG Charts' in-place update. Nothing is
       * destroyed and nothing re-initialises.
       *
       * Reassigning `data` on the way back from the empty state is NOT
       * redundant even when the numbers are identical: the container was 0×0
       * while hidden, and the property assignment is what asks AG Charts to lay
       * out again now that it is not.
       *
       * `label` goes through setAttribute, which esa-chart routes to
       * applyAria() instead of a redraw — the accessible name follows the name
       * the user is typing without repainting the canvas for it.
       */
      const el = ensureChart();
      el.setAttribute('label', chartLabel);
      el.data = rollup.series.map((year) => ({
        // Years are handed to the chart as STRINGS. A category axis given the
        // integer 2023 formats it as a number and renders "2,023" under the bar
        // — a thousands separator inside a year, which reads as a data bug
        // rather than a formatting one. Strings bypass the numeric formatter.
        year: String(year.year),
        value: year.value,
      }));
      el.series = [
        {
          type: 'bar',
          xKey: 'year',
          yKey: 'value',
          // Names the quantity in the tooltip. Falls back to the verb when the
          // measure carries no unit (Percent and Currency measures do not).
          yName: unitTitle || 'Reported',
          // Both numbers are the dataviz skill's fixed mark spec, not taste:
          // a 4px rounded data-end, and bars capped at 24px so a four-year
          // series does not render four slabs filling their whole bands. AG
          // Charts' `width` is a fixed pixel width; at esa-chart's 300px
          // minimum with six categories the band is still wider than 24, so it
          // never overflows its slot.
          cornerRadius: 4,
          width: 24,
        },
      ];
      // The axis title replaces the legend a single series does not get: it is
      // where the unit is stated on the chart itself. Built conditionally rather
      // than set to `undefined`, so a measure with no unit (Percent, Currency)
      // hands AG Charts an axis with no `title` key at all instead of a present
      // key holding nothing.
      const valueAxis: Record<string, unknown> = { type: 'number', position: 'left' };
      if (unitTitle) valueAxis.title = { enabled: true, text: unitTitle };

      // Only `axes` is overridden. esa-chart merges this object OVER everything
      // it computes, so the token-resolved mark colours, the theme params, the
      // forced-colors repaint and the reduced-motion decision all survive.
      el.options = {
        axes: [{ type: 'category', position: 'bottom' }, valueAxis],
      };
    };

    /*
     * `data`, `series` and `options` are PROPERTIES, not attributes — assigning
     * them before the class is registered would write plain expandos onto an
     * HTMLUnknownElement, which Lit's upgrade does not reclaim, so the chart
     * would come up empty with nothing in the console. whenDefined is the gate,
     * and every paint goes through it rather than only the first: it resolves
     * once and then costs a microtask.
     */
    const ready = customElements.whenDefined('esa-chart');

    /*
     * `latest` rather than a captured argument, so a change that lands while the
     * first paint is still queued cannot be overwritten by it. The listener is
     * registered SYNCHRONOUSLY at module evaluation — deferred module scripts
     * all run before DOMContentLoaded, so a controller that dispatches its
     * initial (draft-merged) event on DOMContentLoaded is guaranteed to find
     * this listener already attached. That ordering is the contract; see the
     * note returned to the page controller.
     */
    let latest = JSON.parse(seedJson) as PerformanceMeasureDefinition;
    let timer = 0;

    document.addEventListener(MEASURE_CHANGE_EVENT, (event: Event) => {
      const detail = (event as CustomEvent<MeasureChangeDetail>).detail;
      // Addressed by slug: the event is document-scoped and carries one record,
      // and nothing stops a screen from stacking two of these panels.
      if (!detail?.measure || (slug && detail.measure.slug !== slug)) return;
      latest = detail.measure;
      window.clearTimeout(timer);
      timer = window.setTimeout(() => void ready.then(() => paint(latest)), SETTLE_MS);
    });

    /*
     * First paint, undebounced, through the SAME function every later paint uses.
     * The server already rendered this panel from the seed, so this looks
     * redundant — it is not. It makes the client path the only live answer, so
     * the two copies of the formatting rules above cannot drift into showing two
     * different things; it wires the chart, which the server cannot; and it
     * means the panel is correct even if the controller never dispatches.
     */
    void ready.then(() => paint(latest));
  };

  document.querySelectorAll<HTMLElement>('[data-firma2-rollup-panel]').forEach(setUp);
</script>

<style>
  /* No layout rules for the panel body beyond the `stack` in the markup, which
     restates esa-collapsible's own 1rem column gap for the wrapper this refactor
     had to introduce. Same token, so the rhythm is unchanged. */

  /* `hidden` has to win. The UA stylesheet's [hidden] { display: none } is the
     weakest rule in the cascade, and `.stack` sets display: flex from an author
     stylesheet — so without this the data view would stay visible in its empty
     state. Two classes' worth of specificity, scoped to this panel, rather than
     an !important that would be inherited by anything nested here later. */
  .firma2-measure-rollup [hidden] {
    display: none;
  }

  /* The chart's box is stable before the chart arrives. esa-chart renders its
     own wrap/canvas with an inline height (320px by default) on its first Lit
     update, so the slot is at full height a microtask after the element is
     appended — well before ag-charts-community finishes its dynamic import and
     paints into it. Nothing here reserves a height, because nothing needs to:
     the element sizes itself, and the data it holds never changes that size. */

  .firma2-measure-rollup__note {
    margin: 0;
    /* Primary ink deliberately — see the NOTE comment in the markup. `body-sm`
       is 14px, the house dense floor, and design-principles' rule for small text
       is "darken as you shrink", not grey it out. */
    color: var(--color-content-default, #202020);
  }

  /* THE TABLE'S RULES ARE :global(), AND THAT IS NOT A SHORTCUT.
     Its rows are rebuilt from JavaScript on every paint (they carry per-year
     figures that change with the record), and Astro's scoping is a COMPILE-TIME
     `data-astro-cid-*` attribute stamped onto nodes in the template. A <tr> made
     by document.createElement never receives it, so a scoped `.foo td` rule
     simply would not match — the table would lose its padding and alignment the
     first time the panel updated, and only then. Declaring them global is the
     honest fix; the `firma2-measure-rollup__` prefix is what keeps them from
     reaching anything else on the page. */
  :global(.firma2-measure-rollup__table) {
    width: 100%;
    /* Collapsed so rows are separated by ONE hairline rather than a pair of
       adjacent borders, which reads as a heavier band than the token intends.
       Same call, same reason, as firma2-project-funding's table. */
    border-collapse: collapse;
    /* Type is inherited from the collapsible's body (.typography-body-md). Not
       downshifted: two columns of four rows is not a dense surface. */
  }

  :global(.firma2-measure-rollup__table th),
  :global(.firma2-measure-rollup__table td) {
    text-align: start;
    vertical-align: baseline;
    padding-block: var(--spacing-200, 0.5rem);
    padding-inline: var(--spacing-300, 0.75rem);
  }

  /* Outer columns drop their outer padding so the table's text aligns with the
     disclosure row above it rather than sitting visibly inset from it. */
  :global(.firma2-measure-rollup__table th:first-child),
  :global(.firma2-measure-rollup__table td:first-child) { padding-inline-start: 0; }
  :global(.firma2-measure-rollup__table th:last-child),
  :global(.firma2-measure-rollup__table td:last-child) { padding-inline-end: 0; }

  :global(.firma2-measure-rollup__table thead th) {
    color: var(--color-content-default-secondary, #646464);
    font-weight: var(--font-weight-semibold, 600);
    border-block-end: var(--border-width-default, 1px) solid var(--color-border-default, #cecece);
  }

  /* A row header labels its row; it does not shout over it. Same weight as the
     figure beside it, so the row reads as one line. */
  :global(.firma2-measure-rollup__table tbody th) {
    font-weight: inherit;
  }

  :global(.firma2-measure-rollup__table tbody tr + tr th),
  :global(.firma2-measure-rollup__table tbody tr + tr td) {
    border-block-start: var(--border-width-default, 1px) solid var(--color-border-default-subtle, #d9d9d9);
  }

  /* Right-aligned with tabular figures so place values stack — 1,300 lines up
     under 538 digit for digit. This is the case the dataviz skill reserves
     tabular-nums for (columns that must align vertically); the stat tiles above
     stay proportional, which is why they are not given this class. */
  :global(.firma2-measure-rollup__num) {
    text-align: end;
    font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;
    white-space: nowrap;
    /* Shrink-to-fit under `table-layout: auto` — the standard idiom for "take
       only what the content needs", handing the leftover width to the Year
       column so neither ever wraps. */
    width: 1%;
  }
</style>
